{"id":"5ccc07c8-306c-487a-8d79-f3ad1b1b1750","object":"chat.completion","created":1785019490,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n    id_to_node = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: [] for n in nodes}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is None:\\n            root_id = n[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        else:\\n            children[n[\\\"parent\\\"]].append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    order = []\\n    stack = [(root_id, 0)]\\n    visited = set()\\n    while stack:\\n        nid, state = stack.pop()\\n        if state == 0:\\n            if nid in visited:\\n                continue\\n            visited.add(nid)\\n            stack.append((nid, 1))\\n            for child in reversed(children[nid]):\\n                stack.append((child, 0))\\n        else:\\n            order.append(nid)\\n    subtree_counts = {}\\n    for nid in order:\\n        counts = {}\\n        sym = id_to_node[nid][\\\"symbol\\\"]\\n        counts[sym] = counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n        for cid in children[nid]:\\n            if cid in subtree_counts:\\n                child_counts = subtree_counts[cid]\\n                for s, cnt in child_counts.items():\\n                    counts[s] = counts.get(s, 0) + cnt\\n        subtree_counts[nid] = counts\\n    global_counts = {s: cnt for s, cnt in subtree_counts[root_id].items()}\\n    retained_set = set(id_to_node.keys())\\n    removed = []\\n    depth_map = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n[\\\"depth\\\"] for n in nodes}\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(id_to_node.keys(), key=lambda x: (-depth_map[x], x))\\n    def get_descendants(start):\\n        desc = [start]\\n        stack = [start]\\n        while stack:\\n            n = stack.pop()\\n            for c in children[n]:\\n                desc.append(c)\\n                stack.append(c)\\n        return desc\\n    for nid in sorted_nodes:\\n        if nid not in retained_set:\\n            continue\\n        node = id_to_node[nid]\\n        if not node[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if node[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(c in retained_set for c in children[nid])\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        feasible = True\\n        need_counts = subtree_counts[nid]\\n        for rsym in required:\\n            if global_counts.get(rsym, 0) - need_counts.get(rsym, 0) <= 0:\\n                feasible = False\\n                break\\n        if not feasible:\\n            continue\\n        to_remove = get_descendants(nid)\\n        for did in to_remove:\\n            retained_set.discard(did)\\n        for sym, cnt in need_counts.items():\\n            global_counts[sym] -= cnt\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained_set)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function solve(case) that implements grammar_guided_node_reduction. The case payload contains nodes (list of dicts) with fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. We need to return removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nGoal: Reduce the parse tree by removing removable nodes, but preserve required_symbols and not remove root. Also cannot remove a node with retained children (i.e., if we keep a child, its parent must be retained). The order: deeper nodes first, then by id for tie-break. For each tentative removal, check that all required symbols remain represented in the tree (after removal). Also grammar_valid: we need to consider that after removal, the tree must remain a valid tree? Not specified exactly, but likely we must ensure that children of removed node are reattached to its parent? Or are they removed too? The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". In Perses-style reduction, removing a node may remove its subtree or replace with a child? The prompt says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" And \"Never remove a root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That suggests that if a node is removable and we consider removing it, we also remove its children (since children are part of its subtree). But \"node with retained children\" cannot be removed, meaning if we plan to keep some descendants (because they might be required?), we cannot remove the parent. Actually careful: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That implies we only remove a node if all its descendants are also removed? Or if we decide to keep a child, then parent must stay. The typical algorithm: bottom-up, if a node is removable, we check if its removal (removing the entire subtree) still satisfies required symbols. If yes, we remove it, meaning all its children are also gone. But \"node with retained children\" suggests that if some child is required (or we choose to keep it for some reason), we cannot remove the parent. But since removal is all-or-nothing for the subtree, we would only consider removing a node if its entire subtree is removable. However the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" after removal of the subtree. So we need to check if the set of symbols in the remaining tree (excluding the subtree) still covers required_symbols.\n\nAlso: we need to consider \"replacement candidates\"? Not sure. The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but then the algorithm seems to only delete nodes? Possibly replacement is when we replace a node with one of its children (like a chain). But the \"Never remove a node with retained children\" suggests we cannot remove a node if any child is retained. So if we replace a node with a child, we would remove the parent but keep the child, which would violate that rule. So perhaps replacement is not considered in this simple version. The output only has removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids, so we just decide which nodes to remove and which to keep.\n\nGiven the fixtures, we need to implement the algorithm: bottom-up traversal (deeper first). For each node, if it's removable (removable: true), and not root, and its symbol is not in required_symbols, then we tentatively consider removing it. However we must also check that after removal, all required symbols still appear somewhere in the tree. Also we must ensure that no retained child exists (i.e., if any descendant is retained, we cannot remove the node). Since we are processing bottom-up, we will have already decided about its children. So we can track a set of retained node IDs. Initially all nodes are candidates for retention? Actually we want to minimize tree? The objective is reduction: remove as many nodes as possible while preserving required symbols and grammar validity. So we start with all nodes retained. Then we iterate nodes in order (deeper first, tie by id). For each node, if it is removable and not root and not required symbol and its entire subtree can be removed (i.e., no child is retained), and after removal required symbols still exist elsewhere, then we remove it. That means we mark it as removed, and also remove all its descendants (which we already have as not retained because if any descendant was retained, we couldn't remove). So we can just add the node to removed list, and its descendants are implicitly removed.\n\nBut we need to think about \"grammar_valid\": the tree must remain a valid tree. In a parse tree, each node corresponds to a grammar rule. If we remove a node, its children are also removed, which may break the tree structure. However, typical reduction algorithms (like Perses) remove nodes that are \"redundant\" by checking if the tree still covers the original input or interestingness. Here the \"interestingness\" is represented by required_symbols. The tree after removal must still have all required symbols. That seems to be the only constraint.\n\nAlso, we need to consider ordering: tentative removal order should be the order in which we actually remove nodes. The output \"removed_node_ids\" should be in the order they are removed (the actual removal order). The \"retained_node_ids\" are the ones kept. They should be sorted? The output schema says array of strings, no order specified. But the prompt says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (ascending order), and removed_node_ids should be in the order of removal (the order we processed and removed). We'll implement that.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Build a map from id to node dict.\n- Build children map: for each node, list of child ids.\n- Determine required_set = set(case[\"required_symbols\"]).\n- Identify root: node with parent null. (In fixtures, root id \"root\".)\n- Initialize retained_set = set of all node ids.\n- Initialize removed_list = [].\n- Get list of node ids sorted by depth descending, then id ascending (stable tie). We'll generate a list of (depth, id) and sort.\n- Iterate over nodes in that order:\n   - node = nodes_map[id]\n   - if not node[\"removable\"]: skip (cannot remove)\n   - if node[\"id\"] == root_id: skip\n   - if node[\"symbol\"] in required_set: skip (required symbol cannot be removed)\n   - Check if any child of this node is retained (i.e., child in retained_set). But since we process bottom-up, children have already been processed. If any child is retained, then we cannot remove this node because we would lose that child's subtree. So skip.\n   - Tentatively remove: we need to check if after removing this subtree, all required symbols still exist elsewhere. This means we need to know the set of symbols present in the retained tree without this node's subtree. But the retained_set already excludes nodes that have been removed (including some children). But this node and its children are currently still retained (since we haven't removed them yet). So we need to simulate removal: remove node and all its descendants from retained_set, and check if required symbols are still satisfied by the remaining retained_set. But we need a way to know the symbols in the rest. We could compute the set of symbols in the current retained_set, then subtract the symbols contributed by this node's subtree. However, symbols may be duplicated; the required symbols just need to be present somewhere. So we can compute the set of symbols in the entire tree (all nodes) and know that the subtree's symbols are a subset. But if a required symbol appears only in this subtree, we cannot remove it. So we need to check for each required symbol, is there at least one retained node outside this subtree with that symbol? Since we haven't removed the subtree yet, we need to consider the retained_set after excluding the subtree. We can precompute for each node the set of symbols in its subtree (including itself). Then during iteration, we can compute the union of symbols from all retained nodes, subtract the subtree's symbols, and check if required symbols are still present. That might be costly but nodes count is small (maybe up to a few hundred). Alternatively, we can maintain a global count of occurrences of each symbol among retained nodes. Initially count all symbols. When we consider removing a subtree, we check if for each required symbol, the count minus its count in the subtree is >0. If yes, we proceed with removal and update counts by subtracting subtree counts. That's efficient.\n\nThus we need:\n- For each node, compute total_symbol_count (dictionary of symbol->count) for its subtree (including itself). We'll compute using post-order DFS.\n- Maintain global symbol_counts (all nodes initially).\n- During removal iteration, for each candidate node:\n   - Compute coverage: for each req_sym in required_set, if global_counts[req_sym] - subtree_counts[req_sym] <= 0 then cannot remove.\n   - If feasible, then remove: add node id to removed_list, remove node and all descendants from retained_set, update global_counts by subtracting subtree_counts.\n- Since we process bottom-up, once a node is removed, its children are also removed (they are already processed and may have been considered earlier, but they are now removed from retained_set). We must ensure we don't process nodes that are already removed (skip if id not in retained_set). So at start of loop, check if id in retained_set.\n\nAlso, we must never remove root, required symbol, or a node with retained children. The \"node with retained children\" condition: we can check if any child (direct) is in retained_set. But because we process bottom-up, children may have already been removed or retained. If any child is retained, we cannot remove parent. That matches the rule: we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained. However, note that if a child is removed, then it's not retained. So that's fine.\n\nBut what about grandchildren? If a child is removed, then its children are also removed, so no problem. If a child is retained, then the parent cannot be removed because it would lose that child's subtree. So correct.\n\nNow, about the ordering: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" We do exactly that.\n\nOne nuance: In the fixtures, we have nodes with depth. Some are not removable. The algorithm should produce deterministic results. Let's test mentally with first case:\n\nRoot (depth 0, removable false, symbol root required)\nn1 (depth 1, removable false, symbol s1 required) \nn2 (depth 1, removable true, symbol s2)\nn3 (depth 1, removable false, symbol s0)\nn4 (depth 2, parent n2, removable true, symbol s1 required? Actually s1 is required, but n4 symbol is s1, so it's a required symbol node, thus cannot be removed)\nn5 (depth 2, parent n3, removable false, symbol s2)\nn6 (depth 2, parent n1, removable true, symbol s0)\n\nRequired: root, s1.\n\nSo nodes that are required: root (id root), n1 (s1), n4 (s1). They cannot be removed.\n\nRemovable: n2 (s2, depth1), n4 (s1 but required), n6 (s0, depth2). n5 not removable.\n\nDepth sorted descending: depth2: n4, n5, n6. depth1: n1, n2, n3. depth0: root.\n\nProcess in order:\n- n4: but symbol s1 required, skip.\n- n5: not removable, skip.\n- n6: removable, symbol s0 not required, check children: none (depth2 leaf). Check if removing n6 (and its subtree, just itself) still leaves required symbols? Currently global counts: root (1), n1 (1), n2 (1), n3 (1), n4 (1), n5 (1), n6 (1). Required symbols: root, s1. s1 appears on n1 and n4. After removing n6 (symbol s0), global counts of s0 decreases but not relevant. So removal passes. Also no retained children (none). So remove n6. Update: retained_set remove n6, global_counts subtract subtree counts (s0-1).\n- Next depth1 nodes in order by id: n1 (required skip), n2 (removable, symbol s2 not required). Need to check children: n4 is child of n2, n4 is retained (since required). So n2 has retained child n4 -> cannot remove (rule: never remove node with retained children). So skip.\n- n3: removable false, skip.\n- root skip.\n\nThus removed nodes: [n6]. Retained: all others: sorted: n1,n2,n3,n4,n5,root? But we need to include all retained IDs. Sorted alphabetical? Let's order: n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root. That seems plausible.\n\nBut is that the expected? Not sure. We'll trust algorithm.\n\nNow we need to implement solve(case) returning {\"removed_node_ids\": list, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted list}. Must be deterministic.\n\nEdge cases: If multiple nodes same depth, order by id string. We'll use sorted list of node ids by depth descending, then id.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Parse case[\"nodes\"].\n- Build dict id_to_node.\n- Build children list.\n- Root id: find node with parent None.\n- Build subtree counts: use recursion or iterative DFS. Since tree may not be deep, recursion okay but Python recursion limit might be issue if large; but we can use stack. We'll use recursion with sys.setrecursionlimit or iterative. We'll use iterative post-order to be safe.\n\nWe'll compute:\n- subtree_counts: dict mapping node id -> dict of symbol counts.\n- Also global_counts initially sum of all node symbols.\n\nAlternatively, we can compute subtree_counts by DFS and store.\n\nAlgorithm steps:\n1. Build adjacency list children.\n2. Identify root (parent is None).\n3. Perform post-order traversal to compute subtree counts for each node.\n   - Use stack: push (node, state). state 0 = unprocessed, state 1 = processed children.\n   - For each node, when processing children, after processing, compute its count = count of its own symbol + sum of children's counts.\n4. Initialize global_counts = subtree_counts[root] (same as sum all).\n5. retained_set = set of all node ids.\n6. removed_list = [].\n7. Create list of node ids sorted by depth descending, then id ascending.\n8. For each id in that list:\n   if id not in retained_set: continue\n   node = id_to_node[id]\n   if not node[\"removable\"]: continue\n   if id == root_id: continue\n   if node[\"symbol\"] in required_set: continue\n   # check retained children\n   children = children_map.get(id, [])\n   has_retained_child = any(c in retained_set for c in children)\n   if has_retained_child: continue\n   # check feasibility: for req_sym in required_set, global_counts[req_sym] - subtree_counts[id].get(req_sym,0) > 0\n   feasible = True\n   for sym in required_set:\n       if global_counts.get(sym,0) - subtree_counts[id].get(sym,0) <= 0:\n           feasible = False\n           break\n   if not feasible: continue\n   # remove node and all descendants\n   # We need to also mark descendants as removed. Since we are processing bottom-up, descendants have already been considered; they are still in retained_set. We should remove them from retained_set.\n   # Collect all descendant ids using BFS/DFS.\n   descendents = get_descendants(id) # include self\n   for did in descendents:\n       retained_set.discard(did)\n   # update global_counts by subtracting subtree_counts[id]\n   for sym, cnt in subtree_counts[id].items():\n       global_counts[sym] -= cnt\n   removed_list.append(id)\n9. After loop, retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained_set))\n10. return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_list, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}\n\nNote: The rule \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" is already enforced by checking children. But what about descendants that are not direct children? Since we are removing the entire subtree, if any descendant is retained, that means the node's child is retained? Not necessarily: if a node is retained, its ancestors must be retained because otherwise the tree would be disconnected? Actually if we remove a node, we also remove all its descendants. So if we see that a child is retained, it means the child itself is retained. So we check direct children.\n\nBut consider case where a child is removed but its child (grandchild) is retained? That can't happen because if a child is removed, its entire subtree is removed. So the only way a descendant is retained is if its parent is also retained. So checking direct children suffices.\n\nOne more nuance: \"grammar_valid\" might require that the tree remains a valid parse tree. Our removal algorithm does not check if the resulting tree is valid according to a grammar. However the problem says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but the only constraints given are the ones we implemented. Possibly the grammar validity is ensured by the fact that we only remove nodes that are removable and preserve required symbols. Since we don't have the actual grammar, we assume the condition \"removable\" flag indicates that removal is grammatically allowed. So we rely on that.\n\nAlso, we must not mutate case. We'll work with copies of sets and counts.\n\nNow, we need to write the solve function and wrap it in a JSON with key \"implementation\". The implementation must be Python source as a string.\n\nLet's write the code. We'll include necessary imports. We'll define helper functions inside solve.\n\nPotential issues: recursion depth? We'll use iterative approach. We'll define get_descendants using BFS.\n\nEdge: The nodes list may not be in any order. We'll build a map.\n\nTesting with the given fixture, we need to ensure we produce expected output? Since we don't have expected outputs, we just need to follow algorithm.\n\nLet's produce the implementation.\n\nWrite Python code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    # build maps\n    id_to_node = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is None:\n            root_id = n[\"id\"]\n        else:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    # post-order traversal to compute subtree counts\n    # using stack\n    order = []  # nodes in post-order\n    stack = [(root_id, 0)]  # (node, state)\n    visited = set()\n    while stack:\n        nid, state = stack.pop()\n        if state == 0:\n            if nid in visited:\n                continue\n            visited.add(nid)\n            stack.append((nid, 1))\n            for child in reversed(children[nid]):  # preserve order\n                stack.append((child, 0))\n        else:\n            order.append(nid)\n    # compute subtree_counts\n    subtree_counts = {}\n    for nid in order:\n        counts = {}\n        sym = id_to_node[nid][\"symbol\"]\n        counts[sym] = counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n        for cid in children[nid]:\n            if cid in subtree_counts:\n                child_counts = subtree_counts[cid]\n                for s, cnt in child_counts.items():\n                    counts[s] = counts.get(s, 0) + cnt\n        subtree_counts[nid] = counts\n    # global counts\n    global_counts = {s: cnt for s, cnt in subtree_counts[root_id].items()}\n    # retained set initially all\n    retained_set = set(id_to_node.keys())\n    removed = []\n    # depth mapping\n    depth_map = {n[\"id\"]: n[\"depth\"] for n in nodes}\n    # sorted nodes: depth descending, id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(id_to_node.keys(), key=lambda x: (-depth_map[x], x))\n    # helper to get all descendants\n    def get_descendants(start):\n        desc = [start]\n        stack = [start]\n        while stack:\n            n = stack.pop()\n            for c in children[n]:\n                desc.append(c)\n                stack.append(c)\n        return desc\n    for nid in sorted_nodes:\n        if nid not in retained_set:\n            continue\n        node = id_to_node[nid]\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in required:\n            continue\n        # check retained children\n        has_retained_child = any(c in retained_set for c in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # check feasibility\n        feasible = True\n        need_counts = subtree_counts[nid]\n        for rsym in required:\n            if global_counts.get(rsym, 0) - need_counts.get(rsym, 0) <= 0:\n                feasible = False\n                break\n        if not feasible:\n            continue\n        # remove subtree\n        to_remove = get_descendants(nid)\n        for did in to_remove:\n            retained_set.discard(did)\n        # update global counts\n        for sym, cnt in need_counts.items():\n            global_counts[sym] -= cnt\n        removed.append(nid)\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained_set)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nWe need to import nothing. This uses only standard Python.\n\nNow create the envelope: {\"implementation\": \"<escaped Python source>\"}\n\nWe'll produce valid JSON.\n\nWe need to escape newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\". We'll do that.\n\nLet's write the full response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":5506,"total_tokens":7678,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4723},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}